The Legacy Jetson Nano (4GB) is a classic, accessible entry point for an Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS) and Autonomous Driving educational Proof of Concept (PoC). However, because of its older Maxwell architecture (released in 2019) and limited 0.5 TFLOPS (FP16) compute power, it requires strict engineering constraints to achieve interactive, real-time feedback loop closure. [1, 2, 3, 4] 
------------------------------
## Hardware Capabilities & Performance Caps
When running modern neural networks like YOLOv8 on this specific hardware, you will encounter immediate computational caps:

* 
* YOLOv8n (Nano) Object Detection: 8–12 FPS at a reduced resolution of 320 × 320 or 416 × 416 pixels using TensorRT FP16. Full 640 × 640 inference drops down to roughly 4–6 FPS, which is too laggy for responsive autonomous vehicle control loops. [5] 
* Semantic Segmentation (Lane Detection): 10–15 FPS using lightweight, specialized architectures like Fast-SCNN or BiSeNet exported to TensorRT.
* End-to-End Behavioral Cloning: 20–30+ FPS when deploying minimalist regression networks (like the NVIDIA Dave-2 architecture used in Donkey Car projects) to map camera pixels directly to steering angles.
* 

------------------------------
## Architectural Design Strategies for an Educational PoC
To successfully close the control loop on a physical scale model (such as a 1/10th scale RC car chassis) using the 4GB Nano, implement these architectural strategies:
## 1. Decouple Perception from the Main Control Loop
Never allow your steering and braking control functions to block or wait on the YOLOv8 inference thread. If a model takes 100 milliseconds to process a frame, your vehicle will travel completely blind between updates.

* 
* Run camera acquisition and low-level motor actuation on independent threads or an external microcontroller (like an Arduino or TeensY).
* Store the latest bounding box or lane geometry vectors in a shared thread-safe buffer.
* Run your Proportional-Integral-Derivative (PID) control loops at a fixed, rapid cadence (50–100 Hz), pulling the latest asynchronous spatial variables from that buffer. [6] 
* 

## 2. Model Pipeline Downscaling

* 
* Scale Input Resolutions Down: Train and export your YOLOv8 models natively at 320 × 320 pixels instead of the standard default 640 × 640. This cuts down total floating-point operations by up to 75%.
* Strip Unused Classes: Modify your dataset to include only immediate driving hazards (e.g., Stop Sign, Traffic Light, Pedestrian, Vehicle). Do not use the massive, stock COCO weights.
* 

## 3. Exploit Legacy Optimizations
Because the Maxwell GPU inside the Jetson Nano 4GB lacks dedicated INT8 Tensor Cores, optimizing via standard INT8 quantization will not yield significant speed improvements and can severely degrade model accuracy. Instead: [7] 

* 
* Maximize performance by forcing FP16 half-precision compilation through the TensorRT compiler execution profile.
* Ensure you configure the Jetson Nano into its maximum performance mode via terminal before starting your driving scripts:

sudo nvpmodel -m 0
sudo jetson_clocks

[8] 
* 

